These native codes, including the DeliveryMassager will in general
need to be compiled. There are two common open-source library
dependencies on Linux and its *nix friends: (a) glib2-dev (b) libxml2.
Be sure to install these.
See also README_PORTABILITY.

Instructions:

(1) Define the environment variable $CSIRO_OSTYPE
    for platform specific identification: Linux-i686-unknown, 
    Darwin-Power-Macintosh-unknown (for MacOSX) etc.
    We do 

    [csh]% setenv CSIRO_OSTYPE `uname -smp | sed 's/ /-/g;s/-[^-]*$/-unknown/g'`
    [sh]%  export CSIRO_OSTYPE=`uname -smp | sed 's/ /-/g;s/-[^-]*$/-unknown/g'`

    On common intel Linuxes, you get:  $CSIRO_OSTYPE = Linux-i686-unknown

    Set this variable permanently in your .cshrc or .profile

    With respect to the level of this README_INSTALL, 
    put ./bin/$CSIRO_OSTYPE  in your path: several binaries
    (deliveryMassager, a utility called eclipseXsection, etc)
	end up here.

    If you don't like our choice of CSIRO_OSTYPE (the string hack from % uname -smp)
    set it as you please, but you must have a ./config/Makefile.ostype.$CSIRO_OSTYPE 
    file with sensible choices for your platform. I suggest you copy
    or link to an existing one in the ./config directory for a closely related platform.

(2) At the level of this README, type

    % cd src/lib
    % make make depend fast install
	% cd ../bin
	% make make depend fast install

    which should compile optimised binaries and install everything.
    The binaries will end up in ./bin/$CSIRO_OSTYPE with respect 
    to this level of this README (i.e. at the same level as ./src).
    

    *** I.e. you should put ./bin/$CSIRO_OSTYPE in your path. ****
	Or link to the binaries some other way.

