Compilation issues:

a) Dependencies

    The code uses the standard GNOME xml libraries, and ATLAS, so you need as third party stuff:

    1) libxml2-devel 
        Standard GNOME xml toolkit, defacto 
        on most linux distributions, or google it; at last check -
        http://xmlsoft.org/)
        This distribution will compile against the standard RPM.

    2) ATLAS linear algebra library
        The relevant bits are distributed in this tarball for Intel 32 bit Pentium 4's,
        and Mac OSX (PowerPC), but you will need to substitute the header and library files for different
        architectures by pulling down prebuilt ATLAS tarballs or building your own:
        http://math-atlas.sourceforge.net/
        These pieces go in
            Libraries (lib*.a): ./src/lib/ATLAS/lib/$CSIRO_OSTYPE
            Headers (*.h):      ./src/lib/ATLAS/include/

	    For OSX (Macs), ATLAS also comes as part of the development environment for those
       	who may have trouble building ATLAS on newer macs. Provided you have the
	    development environment and "Accelerate" framework in place, the build will detect
        "Darwin" as part of your $CSIRO_OSTYPE and try to link against these libraries.
        To check if you have this, see if you have the headers cblas.h, clapack.h in
	    /System/Library/Frameworks/vecLib.framework/Headers/



b)
    Makefile targets:

    To clean up...
    % cd src
    % make clean

    Note that 
    % make make depend 
    builds system-dependent makefiles for your platform and should need
    to be done only once. Once these are in place, subsequent builds require only
    % make [fast|all|debug]  [install]

    To compile unoptimised, debuggable or optimised code, various permutations of
    % make all
    % make debug
    % make fast
    should work. Try these if optimisation options give trouble (or better yet, fix
    your platform specific options in ./config/Makefile.ostype.$CSIRO_OSTYPE).



c) Altering the make files:

Any unusual compilation flags appear in Makefile.base at the specific
library or binary node of the src tree. Common (say compiler specific)
flags appear in

./config/Makefile.ostype.$CSIRO_OSTYPE 

These can be rejigged if required, but the standard choices are
probably sane.

If you need to alter some particular Makefile.base, after changes, do a 

% make -f Makefile.base

then the usual (e.g.)

% make depend all install, or
% make depend fast install

afterwards. Individual pieces can be installed with  'make install'
at their respective levels, of course. The binaries won't build
until a successful 'make install' of all the libraries has gone
through also.


